[3522.86, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$\u001b[0m git log --format='%h %ad %s' --date=format:'%H:%M:%S' | head -6; ls -l --time-style=+%H:%M build/exp | awk '{print $6, $7}' | sort | sed -n '1p;$p'; for d in e800b g800b i800a k800a m800b n800b r800b u800b v800b x800_1111 z800_1101; do echo \"$d $(date -u -r build/exp/$d/sta.rpt +%H:%M)\"; done; date -u -r build/noiso400/rep/power.rpt +%H:%M; date -u -r submission/pdf_report/fig/fsm.png +%H:%M; grep -n \"^\\*\\*[0-9][0-9]:[0-9][0-9]\" notes/WORKLOG.md\r\n4fe0298 03:27:25 Checkpoint 4: activity-based power at both frequencies, operand-isolation experiment\r\nb6917e9 03:26:37 Checkpoint 3: 800 MHz implementation (Booth/CSA, 7 ranks), flow knobs per frequency, F14 SAT check, mutation tests, waveform figures\r\n60ed005 03:01:30 Checkpoint 2: 400 MHz implementation, checkers, synthesis, timing, gate-level checks\r\n48c0ffe 03:01:30 Checkpoint 1: toolchain (install script, make targets, Nangate45 slow corner, OpenSTA/Yosys images)\r\n045bcfe 19:04:31 Specification, starter files and agent setup\r\n \r\n03:20 z800_1101\r\ne800b 03:02\r\ng800b 03:03\r\ni800a 03:07\r\nk800a 03:10\r\nm800b 03:11\r\nn800b 03:12\r\nr800b 03:14\r\nu800b 03:16\r\nv800b 03:17\r\nx800_1111 03:19\r\nz800_1101 03:20\r\n03:27\r\n03:28\r\n7:**02:30 Environment survey.** Ubuntu 24.04 x86-64, 4 cores, 15 GB RAM. Preinstalled: Icarus Verilog 12.0, Yosys 0.33 (apt), Verilator 5.020, GTKWave, Xvfb, pdflatex, pandoc. Docker CLI was present, but the daemon was not running; it was started with `dockerd &`. OpenSTA and OpenROAD are not in apt (`apt-cache policy opensta` gives no candidate).\r\n9:**02:34 Nangate45 slow corner.** OpenROAD-flow-scripts ships only `NangateOpenCellLibrary_typical.lib`. The public mirror `mflowgen/freepdk-45nm`, at commit d3be42a7, holds `stdcells-wc.lib`. That file is the Nangate Open Cell Library's slow corner: `operating_conditions (slow)`, process 1.0, 0.95 V, 125 \u00b0C, default wire load `5K_hvratio_1_1`. The repository also has the typical and best-case libraries, so the worst-case file is the slowest corner available. Its `stdcells.v` is the Nangate Verilog cell library, with `specify` blocks, `$setuphold`/`$width` checks and notifier UDPs (250 specify blocks, 196 `$setuphold`). Both files are fetched by `submission/scripts/install.sh` and checked by sha256.\r\n11:**02:36 OpenSTA / Yosys images.** Docker Hub has `openroad/opensta` (OpenSTA 3.1.0, f8e13cbf) and `openroad/orfs` (Yosys 0.68, OpenROAD). Both are pinned by digest in `install.sh` and in the `Makefile`. Yosys 0.68 was chosen over apt's 0.33 because it has `synth -booth` and the parallel-prefix `$lcu` maps (`+/choices/kogge-stone.v`). OpenROAD, from the same image, is used only for the Resizer (gate sizing).\r\n13:**02:38 Does the simulator turn timing violations into X?** No. A test of `DFF_X1` with D changing 50 ps before CK (setup 100 ps), under `iverilog -gspecify`, captured the new value with no X (`/tmp/claude-0/probe/tc/t.v`). Icarus 12 parses `$setuphold` but does not evaluate it, so the notifier never toggles. OSS CVC, an open-source simulator that does evaluate timing checks, would have to be built from source, and building it was blocked by this session's permission policy (external code). Decision: use Icarus. Under SPEC 8.2's fallback, the gate-level checkers detect violations through incorrect values; Icarus does apply SDF IOPATH and INTERCONNECT delays, so a late D is captured stale. The report describes this limitation.\r\n15:**02:40 install.sh.** It installs the apt packages, starts dockerd if needed, pulls both images by digest, fetches and checksums the library, and writes `submission/syn_reports/tool_versions.txt`. Run: `bash submission/scripts/install.sh` gave \"install complete\".\r\n23:**02:42 Checkers delegated.** A subagent wrote `rtl_checker.sv`, `sdf_checker_400.sv` and `sdf_checker_800.sv` from SPEC.md, without access to `submission/rtl_*`. It smoke-tested them against its own throwaway behavioural model and 12 broken models; all were caught.\r\n30:**02:45 First synthesis.** Yosys default (`synth`, `abc -D`) on the simple 2-stage RTL (operand register, then the whole multiply, then the product register): 1772 \u00b5m\u00b2, but WNS \u22125.6 ns. The critical path was 8 ns: a ripple-like final adder, plus a DFF_X1 at fanout 29 with 0.99 ns clk\u2192Q. At this corner (SS, 0.95 V, 125 \u00b0C, 5K wire load) a gate costs 0.1\u20130.5 ns.\r\n32:**02:50 Flow improvements, measured on the same RTL.**\r\n37:**03:00 Own multiplier `mult_pipe.sv`.** It uses radix-4 modified Booth with 8 partial products. Sign extension is replaced by inverted sign bits plus the constant 0xAAAB0000, and each row's +1 sits in the free bit of the next row. Carry-save 3:2 levels go 9\u21926\u21924\u21923\u21922, then a final adder. Every pipeline cut is a parameter (PRE_ENC, ENC_REG, CSA_REG[3:0], CPA_SPLIT). Verified standalone against `a*b` over 200k random and corner pairs in several configurations (0 errors), and inside mkMultBuff with the independent RTL checker in 4 configurations (all ALL PASS).\r\n39:**03:05 Sweep of the 400 MHz cut** (`build/exp/run.sh`; Yosys + Resizer + OpenSTA; area is post-Resizer):\r\n56:**03:15 Full 400 MHz flow (`make synth|check|sim|timing|power RUN=400`).**\r\n63:**03:20 Netlists that must fail (`make timing_fail RUN=400|800`).**\r\n68:**03:25 Power (`make power RUN=400`).** OpenSTA `read_vcd -scope mkMultBuff_tb/DUT -begin_time/-end_time` on the zero-delay gate-level VCD:\r\n79:**03:28 Mutation test of the RTL checker (`submission/scripts/mutate.sh 400`).** 11 one-line mutants of the real RTL, covering ready/valid off-by-ones, missing resets, ignored enables and wrong data source. All 11 were killed (`submission/syn_reports/400/mutation.log`).\r\n81:**03:30 check/400 pushed** (`git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/check/400`, commit 60ed005). Work continued without waiting. Note: `rtl_800/` was still empty at that point, so the 800 part of that scorecard is expected to fail.\r\n85:**03:32 First 800 MHz probes** (`build/exp/run.sh`, mult_pipe configurations at 1.25 ns): WNS between \u22121.1 and \u22122.8 ns. The critical path was the `RST_N` input: one DFF_X1-modelled driver into 90 to 174 synchronous-reset gates, 1.4 to 2.6 ns. The Resizer cannot buffer it without placement (RSZ-0075 makeBufferedNet failed), and ABC's `buffer -p` changed nothing (the results were bit-identical).\r\n88:**03:40 Parallel candidate evaluations (two subagents) while I iterated on the Booth/CSA candidate:**\r\n119:**04:20 rtl_800 = z800_1101** (latency 7, 577 flops; config in `submission/rtl_800/mkMultBuff.sv`). It meets the 62 ps target at the lowest area of the passing variants. The 1111 variant has more slack (+0.150 ns) but costs +456 \u00b5m\u00b2 and about +0.34 mW of vectorless power.\r\n133:**04:35 Candidate (C), Baugh-Wooley + Dadda (subagent, `build/cand_dadda/`).**\r\n152:**04:40 Mutation test at 800 MHz** (`submission/scripts/mutate.sh 800`): 13/13 mutants killed. The extra mutants vs 400 are EN_writeMem one cycle early, the inverted port copy not reset, and a carry-select carry-in error in mult_pipe.\r\n154:**04:45 Waveform figures** (`submission/scripts/waves/waves.sh 400|800`). The same gate-level checker drives the RTL and the SDF-annotated netlist, so the waves line up. GTKWave 3.3.116 runs under `xvfb-run` with `-S` Tcl scripts (`/File/Grab_To_File`), light colours set by `--rcvar`, and ImageMagick crops. Windows: first writes, transition to drain, end of drain, and a 3-cycle zoom.\r\n"]
[3533.761, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$\u001b[0m python3 - <<'EOF'\r\nimport re\r\np='notes/WORKLOG.md'\r\ns=open(p).read()\r\ns=re.sub(r'^\\*\\*\\d\\d:\\d\\d ', '**', s, flags=re.M)\r\ns=s.replace(\"Times are UTC on 2026-11-08. Each entry gives what was done, why, the commands and the results.\",\r\n\"\"\"Entries are in chronological order on 2026-11-08 (UTC). An earlier draft of this log carried clock times on each entry that were estimates rather than readings, and they were wrong, so they were removed. The reliable times come from git and from file timestamps:\r\n- session start 02:33\r\n- checkpoints 1 and 2 committed 03:01, with check/400 pushed right after\r\n- 800 MHz iterations 03:02\u201303:20 (`build/exp/*/sta.rpt`)\r\n- checkpoint 3 committed 03:26, with check/800 pushed right after\r\n- checkpoint 4 committed 03:27\r\n\r\nEach entry gives what was done, why, the commands and the results.\"\"\")\r\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\r\nEOF\r\ngrep -c \"^\\*\\*[0-9][0-9]:\" notes/WORKLOG.md; sed -n 1,12p notes/WORKLOG.md\r\n0\r\n# Work log: mkMultBuff (400 MHz and 800 MHz)\r\n\r\nEntries are in chronological order on 2026-11-08 (UTC). An earlier draft of this log carried clock times on each entry that were estimates rather than readings, and they were wrong, so they were removed. The reliable times come from git and from file timestamps:\r\n- session start 02:33\r\n- checkpoints 1 and 2 committed 03:01, with check/400 pushed right after\r\n- 800 MHz iterations 03:02\u201303:20 (`build/exp/*/sta.rpt`)\r\n- checkpoint 3 committed 03:26, with check/800 pushed right after\r\n- checkpoint 4 committed 03:27\r\n\r\nEach entry gives what was done, why, the commands and the results. Tool logs that support reported numbers are kept under `submission/syn_reports/`.\r\n\r\n## Checkpoint 1: toolchain\r\n"]
[3551.989, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$\u001b[0m cat > submission/pdf_report/src/signoff.md <<'EOF'\r\nThe sign-off service of SPEC Section 8.3 synthesises the submitted RTL with the GPDK045 library and simulates the netlists with SDF back-annotation against its own testbench, then publishes a scorecard on the branch `check-results`. Two submissions were made. `check/400` (commit 60ed005) carried the 400 MHz design while `rtl_800/` was still empty, and `check/800` (commit b6917e9) carried both final designs, which are unchanged since.\r\n\r\nAt the time this report was built, the branch `check-results` did not exist on the remote (`git fetch origin check-results` returned \"couldn't find remote ref\"), so no GPDK045 scorecard was available to compare with. The service is described as answering within 5 to 20 minutes while it is online, and the absence of any result branch suggests it was offline during the session. The comparison therefore cannot be made here. The open-source results above are complete and stand on their own.\r\n\r\nSome differences should be expected once a scorecard is available. GPDK045 sign-off runs at its own corner, typically nominal, while this flow uses the Nangate45 slow corner at 0.95 V and 125 \u00b0C, where gates are roughly two to three times slower. So the GPDK045 slack of both designs should be much larger, and the 800 MHz design, sized for the slow corner, should close with a wide margin. GPDK045 also has different flip-flops and a different synthesis tool, so area and power will not match the Nangate figures cell for cell. The functional results should agree, since both implementations are fully synchronous and every output except `EN_readMem` and `memVal_data` comes from a register.\r\nEOF\r\nbash submission/scripts/build_report.sh 2>&1 | tail -5\r\nreport written: submission/pdf_report/report.pdf (Pages:           49)\r\n"]
[3559.685, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$\u001b[0m mkdir -p build/report/pages && pdftoppm -r 50 -png -f 1 -l 12 submission/pdf_report/report.pdf build/report/pages/p && ls build/report/pages | head -20 && cd build/report/pages && convert p-01.png p-02.png p-03.png p-04.png +append row1.png && convert p-05.png p-06.png p-07.png p-08.png +append row2.png && convert p-09.png p-10.png p-11.png p-12.png +append row3.png\r\np-01.png\r\np-02.png\r\np-03.png\r\np-04.png\r\np-05.png\r\np-06.png\r\np-07.png\r\np-08.png\r\np-09.png\r\np-10.png\r\np-11.png\r\np-12.png\r\n"]
[3591.804, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$\u001b[0m ls q* | head -3; convert q-01.png q-02.png q-03.png q-04.png +append r1.png && convert q-05.png q-06.png q-07.png q-08.png +append r2.png\r\nq-01.png\r\nq-02.png\r\nq-03.png\r\n"]
[3600.647, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$\u001b[0m cd /home/user/elec402-p2-open/submission/pdf_report/src && python3 - <<'EOF'\r\nfor f,pairs in [('dp400.dot',[('enc','r1'),('c12','r2'),('cpa','r3')]),('dp800.dot',[('enc','r1'),('pp','r2'),('c1','r3'),('c23','r4'),('c4','r5'),('ca','r6'),('cb','r7')])]:\r\n    s=open(f).read().rstrip().rstrip('}')\r\n    s=s.replace('rankdir=TB','rankdir=TB; newrank=true')\r\n    for a,b in pairs:\r\n        s+=f'  {{rank=same; {a}; {b};}}\\n'\r\n    s+='}\\n'\r\n    open(f,'w').write(s)\r\ns=open('dp400.dot').read()\r\n# in dp400 'pp' and 'c34' are extra logic boxes: put them on their own rows\r\nopen('dp400.dot','w').write(s.replace('  {rank=same; c12; r2;}','  {rank=same; c12; r2;}\\n  {rank=same; pp;}'))\r\nEOF\r\nfor f in dp400 dp800; do dot -Tpng -Gdpi=150 $f.dot -o ../fig/$f.png; done; identify ../fig/dp400.png ../fig/dp800.png\r\n../fig/dp400.png PNG 977x902 977x902+0+0 8-bit sRGB 95833B 0.000u 0:00.000\r\n../fig/dp800.png PNG 1243x1050 1243x1050+0+0 8-bit sRGB 105401B 0.000u 0:00.000\r\n"]
